---
// The terms. Two different things are covered and they are kept apart on purpose: the software,
// which is AGPL and carries the licence's terms, and the services we run, which are ours.
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const UPDATED = "22 September 2026";
---

<Layout
  title="Terms · Surfing Dog"
  description="The licence on the software, and the terms of the services we run: this website, the requests board, and the directory when it opens."
>
  <section class="wrap wrap-read section is-head" id="terms" aria-labelledby="terms-h">
    <p class="eyebrow">Terms</p>
    <h1 class="h-page" id="terms-h">The software, and the services</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Two different things, with different rules. The software is open source and yours to run. The services we
      operate are ours to run, and these are the terms for them.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">Last updated {UPDATED}. Surfing Dog Lda, Portugal. Questions: hello@surfingdog.ai</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="sw-h">
    <div class="wrap wrap-read prose">
      <h2 class="h-section" id="sw-h">The software</h2>
      <p>
        Surfing Dog Inbox is licensed under the <b>GNU Affero General Public License v3.0</b>. You may run it, read
        it, change it and share it. If you run a modified version as a service for other people, the licence asks you
        to offer them your changes. Three parts are MIT instead, so that speaking to an inbox costs no one a copyleft
        obligation: the open formats, the client SDK, and the connector SDK.
      </p>
      <p>
        The licence text governs, and it is in the repository. It includes what every open-source licence includes:
        the software comes with no warranty, and the authors are not liable for what happens when you run it. If you
        run it, you are the one running it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">The services we run</h2>
      <p>
        This website, the documentation, the requests board and the directory are services we operate. Using them
        means accepting what follows. If you do not, do not use them; the software works without any of them.
      </p>

      <h3 class="h-card">What you may not do</h3>
      <ul class="checks">
        <li>Post anything unlawful, or anything you have no right to post.</li>
        <li>Impersonate somebody else, or claim to speak for a business that is not yours.</li>
        <li>Attack the services, or try to reach data that is not yours.</li>
        <li>Scrape at a rate that degrades the service for other people.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We may remove a post or refuse service for these reasons. If we do, and you ask, we will tell you which
        reason it was.
      </p>

      <h3 class="h-card">The requests board</h3>
      <p>
        What you post there is public and stays public. You keep ownership of what you write and give us permission
        to show it on the site. Votes decide what we look at first; they are not a promise that anything gets built,
        and nothing said there is a commitment to ship.
      </p>

      <h3 class="h-card">Availability</h3>
      <p>
        These services are offered as they are, with no uptime guarantee and no warranty. They are free, and we may
        change or withdraw them. If something goes down, we fix it because we use it too, not because a contract says
        we must.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">Hosting, when it opens</h2>
      <p>
        Paid hosted tenancy is not open. When it is, it gets its own agreement with the things a paid service needs
        and this page does not have: what the service promises, what it costs, how to leave and take your data with
        you, and a written data processing agreement. Nothing on this page is an offer to sell anything.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">Liability</h2>
      <p>
        To the extent the law allows, we are not liable for indirect or consequential loss, for lost profit, or for
        lost data, arising from the free services or from the software. Nothing here limits liability that cannot be
        limited, including for death or personal injury caused by negligence, or for fraud. If you are a consumer,
        your statutory rights are unaffected.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h-section">Law</h2>
      <p>
        Portuguese law, and the courts of Portugal. If you are a consumer in the European Union, you keep the
        protection of the law where you live and may bring a claim there.
      </p>

      <div class="glass card" style="margin-top: var(--sp-6)">
        <h3 class="h-card">A note on where this stands</h3>
        <p class="card-text">
          Written plainly by the people who built the thing, and not yet reviewed by a lawyer. The review happens
          before anything is sold, and this notice comes off the page when it has.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
